        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          <B>Objectives:</B><P>Long duration space flight missions will require a renewable source of food and an efficient method to process and recycle many waste materials. Plants provide an obvious solution to these problems. However, plant growth and reproduction in space have always been problematic. This experiment was designed to grow Super-Dwarf wheat on the Russian Mir space station through its entire life cycle.<P><B>Approach:</B><P>Hardware was transported to Mir and set up in July 1996. Seeds were planted on August 5, 1996. Plants were sampled at 21, 39, 52, and 63 days after planting. Samples were placed into a chemical fixative and placed into a desiccant bag. A second planting was made on December 6, 1996. CO2 and water vapor were measured and recorded. Several plants were sampled using the same procedure on December 24. After thirty-nine days of growth, the plants were harvested and transported back to Earth in the GN2 freezer aboard the space shuttle Atlantis.<P> <B>Results:</B><P>Plants in the both batches appeared healthy and produced a considerable amount of biomass in comparison to previous experiments. The first planting produced about 280 heads by the time that the plants were harvested on December 6. However, the heads contained no seeds and the flower either had not formed pollen or had not released it. Further, the pollen grains contained only one or two nuclei and were probably sterile. Despite differences in growth rate, every plant appeared to have been arrested at the stage in growth just before pollen release. These defects could be a possible effect of elevated ethylene (C2H2) in the atmosphere of Mir and are consistent with the know effects of ethylene on plants. Both plantings were found to have elevated levels of calcium, boron, aluminum, and cadmium, while there were magnesium and phosphorous deficiencies in both sets of plants.
        </div>

